      "role": "user",
      "content": "yo i keep getting an 'Access Denied' error when my lambda trying to write logs to s3 even though i think the policy is right. can u look at this?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hey there! That \"Access Denied\" error is definitely giving you a headache, but don't worry, we can solve this together. It's a bit like trying to sneak into a locked room without a key; sometimes the key is there, but the lock is just a bit picky about how you present it.\n\nHere's the thing: your policy looks mostly fine, but S3 is a bit like a strict librarian who only lets you in if you're wearing the right uniform and have the right ID badge. Your current policy allows `s3:PutObject` on `my-app-logs-bucket`, which is good, but there are a few hidden traps that might be tripping you up.\n\nFirst, check if your Lambda execution role actually has the permissions to assume the role that holds this policy. Sometimes the role is like a middleman; if the middleman doesn't have the key to the door, the door stays shut no matter what.\n\nSecond, make sure the bucket name in your policy matches exactly what you're trying to write to. S3 is very literal about names, so a tiny typo is like trying to open a door with the wrong handle—it just won't budge.\n\nThird,",
